      "@type": "rkaf:SourceFragment",
      "oa:hasSource": "urn:rulespec:document-understanding:document:2f1b8c8b8a8e5f20335289c9791b49c29e25931f2eb629b02018723fe3ee8008",
      "rkaf:fragmentIdentityScheme": "rkaf:carrier-local-fragment",
      "rkaf:sourceArtifactDigest": "sha256:2f1b8c8b8a8e5f20335289c9791b49c29e25931f2eb629b02018723fe3ee8008",
      "rkaf:fragmentContentDigest": "sha256:559556255c20c0c2c5f303377edf4e5a723139e9847e40c341cd620d09000d19",
      "rkaf:selectorKind": [
        "oa:TextQuoteSelector",
        "oa:TextPositionSelector"
      ],
      "oa:hasSelector": [
        {
          "@type": "oa:TextQuoteSelector",
          "oa:exact": "b. Prior to November 9, 1989, consular officers, when\r\nrequested to do so, could make certain that foreign marriages were effective\r\nfor purposes of U.S. law by acting as official witnesses to the marriage, when\r\none of the contracting parties was a U.S. citizen, pursuant to the authority of\r\nSection 31 of the Act of June 22, 1860 (12 Stat. 79; 22 U.S.C. 4192). The Act\r\nalso provided for issuance of an official certificate of witness to such\r\nmarriage."
        },
        {
          "@type": "oa:TextPositionSelector",
          "oa:start": 311,
          "oa:end": 767,
          "rkaf:coordinateSystem": "rkaf:unicode-codepoint"
        }
      ]
    },
